  publication_type = {collaboration},
  keywords = {Neutron stars, Supernova remnants, Gravitational waves, Gravitational wave astronomy, 1108, 1667, 678, 675, General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology, High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena},
  abstract = {{We present results from directed searches for continuous gravitational
waves from a sample of 15 nearby supernova remnants, likely
hosting young neutron star candidates, using data from the first
eight months of the fourth observing run (O4) of the
LIGO─Virgo─KAGRA Collaboration. The analysis employs five
pipelines: four semicoherent methods{\textemdash}the Band-
Sampled-Data directed pipeline, Weave, and two Viterbi pipelines
(single- and dual-harmonic){\textemdash}and PyStoch, a cross-
correlation-based pipeline. These searches cover wide frequency
bands and do not assume prior knowledge of the targets'
ephemerides. No evidence of a signal is found from any of the 15
sources. We set 95\% confidence-level upper limits on the
intrinsic strain amplitude, with the most stringent constraints
reaching {\ensuremath{\sim}}4 {\texttimes}
10$^{{\ensuremath{-}}26}$ near 300 Hz for the nearby source
G266.2{\ensuremath{-}}1.2 (Vela Jr.). We also derive limits on
neutron star ellipticity and r-mode amplitudes for the same
source, with the best constraints reaching
{\ensuremath{\lesssim}}10$^{{\ensuremath{-}}7}$ and
{\ensuremath{\lesssim}}10$^{{\ensuremath{-}}5}$, respectively,
at frequencies above 400 Hz. For frequencies above 200 Hz, these
results represent the most sensitive wide-band directed searches
for continuous gravitational waves from supernova remnants to
date.}},
