  abstract = {{Version 4.0 of the Gravitational-Wave Transient Catalog (GWTC-4.0) adds
new candidates detected by the LIGO, Virgo, and KAGRA
observatories through the first part of the fourth observing run
(O4a: 2023 May 24 15:00:00 to 2024 January 16 16:00:00 UTC) and
a preceding engineering run. In these new data, we find 128
compact binary coalescence candidates that are identified by at
least one of our search algorithms with a probability of
astrophysical origin p$_{astro}$ {\ensuremath{\geq}} 0.5 and
that are not vetoed during event validation. We also provide
detailed source property measurements for 86 of these that have
a false-alarm rate <1 yr$^{{\ensuremath{-}}1}$. Based on the
inferred component masses, these candidates are consistent with
signals from binary black holes (BBHs) and neutron star─black
hole binaries (GW230518\_125908 and GW230529\_181500). Median-
inferred component masses of BBHs in the catalog now range from
5.79M{\ensuremath{\odot}} (GW230627\_015337) to
137M{\ensuremath{\odot}} (GW231123\_135430), while
GW231123\_135430 was probably produced by the most massive
binary observed in the catalog. For the first time, we have
discovered BBH signals with network signal-to-noise ratio
exceeding 30, GW230814\_230901 and GW231226\_101520, enabling
high-fidelity studies of the waveforms and astrophysical
properties of these systems. Combined with the 90 candidates
included in GWTC-3.0, the catalog now contains 218 candidates
with p$_{astro}$ {\ensuremath{\geq}} 0.5 and not otherwise
vetoed, more than doubling the size of the catalog and further
opening our view of the gravitational-wave Universe.}},
